Why Garage Doors Break Down More Than You'd Expect
A garage door might look simple from the outside, but it's actually a coordinated system of springs, cables, rollers, tracks, and a motorized opener, all working together every time you press that button. With daily use, averaging two or more cycles a day for most households, it's no surprise that something eventually wears out.
Georgia's climate adds its own wear and tear to the mix. Humid summers can cause wood panels to swell or warp, while sudden temperature swings in fall and winter can affect metal components and lubrication. Add in the occasional severe storm, and it's easy to see why garage door repair Columbus, GA searches tend to spike after major weather events.

Common Problems and What They Usually Mean
The door won't open or close at all. This is often traced back to a broken spring, a dead opener battery, or a sensor that's become misaligned. Garage door openers use safety sensors near the bottom of the track, and even a small bump can knock them out of position.
The door is noisy or jerky when moving. Grinding, squeaking, or uneven movement usually points to worn rollers, a lack of lubrication, or a track that's slightly bent out of shape. These issues tend to get worse gradually, so catching them early can prevent a bigger repair down the line.
The door feels unusually heavy. If you're opening the door manually and it feels like dead weight, there's a good chance a spring has lost tension or broken. Springs are designed to counterbalance the door's weight, so when they fail, the full weight falls on the opener motor or on you.
The remote or keypad isn't working. Sometimes the fix is as simple as replacing a battery or reprogramming the remote, but if multiple remotes stop working at once, the opener's receiver itself may need attention.

Safe DIY Fixes vs. Calling a Professional
Plenty of garage door maintenance is well within reach for most homeowners. Lubricating hinges, rollers, and tracks every few months helps reduce wear and keeps the door running smoothly. Tightening visibly loose bolts, clearing 
debris from the tracks, and testing the sensors are also simple tasks that don't require special tools or training.
That said, certain repairs come with real risk attached. Garage door springs are under significant tension, and attempting to replace or adjust them without proper equipment can cause serious injury. Issues involving the opener's electrical wiring also fall into this category. If a repair involves springs, cables, or the internal components of the opener motor, it's generally safer and more cost-effective in the long run to call a licensed technician.

